1 COPY

   Copies a file, or files a wildcard matches, from one place on a
   mounted volume to another, or between a volume and the host's file
   system.

   Format

     COPY input-filespec output-filespec

2 Parameters

 input-filespec

   The file to copy.  On a volume, wildcards may match several files
   when the output names a directory.  With /HOST after it, the input is
   a host file system path.

 output-filespec

   Where the copy goes: a file, or a directory.  With /HOST after it,
   the output is a host file system path.

   An output that names no file of its own (a directory, or a host
   directory) takes each input file's name and type.  On a volume, a
   name or type written as * is the input file's: COPY X.MAR * keeps
   X.MAR's name and type, and COPY *.TXT [SAVE]*.OLD copies each .TXT
   file to an .OLD file of the same name.  No other wildcards are
   allowed in the output.

2 Description

   Each side of a copy is a volume or the host, decided by itself: a
   name such as DUA0:[DIR]NAME.TYPE is on a mounted volume, and /HOST
   after a parameter makes it a host path instead.  A name with a host
   path ("./notes.txt", quoted to keep its case) is a host file too, and
   so is a bare name when the default directory isn't on a volume.

   A file copied to a volume gets the version its output gives (COPY
   V.OBJ Z.OBJ;7 makes Z.OBJ;7), or the next version when it gives none
   or ;0.  An output version of ;* keeps each input file's version.  COPY
   never writes over an existing version:

     %COPY-E-OPENOUT, error opening DUA0:[WORK]Z.OBJ;7 as output
     -RMS-E-FEX, file already exists, not superseded

   Unless /BINARY is given, a file is copied as lines of text: a volume
   file's records, whatever their format, become lines on the host, and
   a host file becomes a Stream_LF file on the volume, unless
   /RECORD_FORMAT asks for another kind.  A copy from one place on a
   volume to another keeps the file's record format and attributes.

   An object module (.OBJ) is always copied as records.  On the host it
   is kept as ODS-2 keeps variable-length records on disk (each record a
   2-byte length and its bytes, padded to an even length), so its
   records survive a copy to the host and back.  A host .OBJ copied to a
   volume gets the attributes VAX MACRO gives an object module.

2 Qualifiers
/BINARY

   Copies the bytes as they are, with no records and no line endings
   changed.

/CARRIAGE_CONTROL

      /CARRIAGE_CONTROL=keyword

   With /RECORD_FORMAT, the records' carriage control: CARRIAGE_RETURN,
   FORTRAN, NONE, or for VFC records PRINT.  The default is
   CARRIAGE_RETURN for lines (PRINT for VFC) and NONE with /BINARY.

/CONTROL_FIELD_SIZE

      /CONTROL_FIELD_SIZE=n

   With /RECORD_FORMAT=VFC, the number of control bytes in each record.
   The default is 2.

/CRLF

   For a host output, ends each line with a carriage return and a line
   feed.  /CRLF and /LF can't both be given.

/DIRS

   For a host output, makes each matched directory an empty host
   directory, and puts each matched file under the same subdirectories
   it has on the volume, rather than all of them in the output
   directory.

/HOST

   After a parameter, makes it a host file system path, whatever it
   looks like.  Each parameter has its own /HOST.

/IGNORE

   For a host output, goes on past a record that can't be read by
   copying the rest of the file as bytes, rather than failing.

/LF

   For a host output, ends each line with a line feed.  This is the
   default.

/LOG

      /LOG

   Shows each file copied by its full name, the output file's too, with
   the number of blocks copied, in place of the short confirmation.

/QUIET

   Leaves out the %COPY-S-COPIED message for each file.

/RECORD_FORMAT

      /RECORD_FORMAT=keyword

   For a host input, the file to make on the volume, since a host file
   can't say what records it holds.  Without /BINARY the host file is
   read as lines, a record each; with /BINARY it is read as bytes.  The
   keyword is:

     FIXED      Fixed-length records of /RECORD_SIZE bytes.  Lines are
                padded with blanks; bytes are cut, and must make whole
                records.
     VARIABLE   Variable-length records.  Bytes are cut every
                /RECORD_SIZE bytes.
     VFC        Variable-length records with a fixed control field.
                Lines only.
     STREAM, STREAM_LF, STREAM_CR
                Stream files, each line ending with a carriage return and
                line feed, a line feed, or a carriage return.  Bytes are
                kept as they are.
     UNDEFINED  No records.  Needs /BINARY; the bytes are kept as they
                are.

   This qualifier is govax's own.

/RECORD_SIZE

      /RECORD_SIZE=n

   With /RECORD_FORMAT, a fixed record's length, or the longest record
   allowed, up to 32767.

/STREAM

   For a host output, copies a stream file's bytes as they are, rather
   than making its line endings the host's.

/TEST

   Shows what would be copied, and copies nothing.

/TIME

   For a host output, gives the host file the volume file's revision
   date as its modification time.

/VERBOSE

   Shows a %COPY-I-COPYING message before each file is copied.

/VFC

   Taken, and does nothing: a VFC file's carriage control is always
   expanded when it's copied as text.

2 Examples

   1.$ COPY FOO.TXT BAR.TXT

     Copies FOO.TXT in the default directory to BAR.TXT, a new version
     if BAR.TXT exists.

   2.$ COPY "notes.txt"/HOST NOTES.TXT

     Copies the host file notes.txt onto the volume, as a Stream_LF
     file.

   3.$ COPY DUA1:[WORK]*.MAR "save"/HOST

     Copies each .MAR file in [WORK] to the host directory save.

   4.$ COPY/BINARY/RECORD_FORMAT=FIXED/RECORD_SIZE=512 -
     _$ "image.dat"/HOST IMAGE.DAT

     Copies a host file's bytes into a file of 512-byte fixed records.
